\series default
 the probability assumed by a qubit must be something repeatable in time without any doubt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physic behind quantum computing use wave forms that give us the probability to have a qubit in a certain state,
 but when we made a measurements of the qubit the state collapse and we obtain as outcome of a measurement a random number,
 with the probabilities given by the squared amplitudes.
 What is deterministic is the evolution between measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
It is often stated that,
 unlike classical physics,
 Quantum Physics is not deterministic.
 This statement is not really correct,
 but we need to define carefully what can and what cannot be determined.
 Very simply,
 this means that we can only determine the properties of a system that the system actually possesses (pretty obvious really!).
 In classical mechanics,
 given an initial condition,
 defined by the exact position and momentum of a particle at some time,
 Newton’s laws of motion provide us with a set of equations,
 whose solution will determine the position and momentum of that particle at any subsequent time.
 The reason why this determination is not possible in Quantum Physics is simply because Heisenberg’s Uncertainty relation prevents us from realizing the required initial condition.
 Remember that this uncertainty relation tells us that the particle does not possess well-defined position and momentum,
 so it is meaningless to ask whether we can determine them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
On the other hand,
 if a system is known to be in a certain quantum state,
 described by a wavefunction at time t1,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathit{\Psi(x,t1)}$
\end_inset

,
 then the solution to Schrödinger’s equation gives us exactly the wavefunction,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathit{\Psi(x,t2)}$
\end_inset

,
 at a later time t2 – and therefore (provided we can solve Schrödinger’s equation) we know the quantum state of the system at any later time.
\end_layout

